<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Setúbal</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Setúbal is a port city on the estuary of the Sado in southern Portugal, south of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lisbon">Lisbon</a>. In Roman times the area was a centre of fish-salting, and at Tróia, across the estuary, archaeologists have excavated one of the largest fish-salting complexes known in the Roman world, which exported salted fish and sauces. The modern city is a centre of fishing, salt-making, industry and shipping.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/setubal.jpg" alt="Setúbal" width="320" height="211" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Filipe Rocha,sacavem (pt) (pt:usuário:sacavem) · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Setubal_III_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Setúbal stands on the northern bank of the Sado estuary, about 50 km by road from central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lisbon">Lisbon</a>, and belongs to the Lisbon metropolitan area. The Arrábida hills begin at its western beaches, and the Tróia peninsula lies across the river. In the time of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-andalus">Al-Andalus</a> it was called Shaṭūbar in Andalusian Arabic, after the older pre-Roman name Caetobriga. English traders of the 17th century called the port Saint Ubes.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Remains of human occupation in the municipality date from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neolithic">Neolithic</a> onwards. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phoenicians">Phoenicians</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthaginians</a> came to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberia">Iberia</a> in search of salt and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tin">tin</a>, and in Roman times the settlement developed fish-salting factories and pottery kilns. No records survive for the period between the 6th and 12th centuries. After the Christians took Alcácer do Sal in 1217, Setúbal came under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/order-of-santiago">Order of Santiago</a>, which granted it a municipal charter in March 1249. In 1343 an agreement settled its territory against its neighbours Palmela and Alcácer do Sal, and a wall was built.</p><p>In the 15th century shipbuilding and maritime trade brought wealth, and in 1458 Afonso V sailed from its port to conquer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ksar-es-seghir">Ksar es-Seghir</a>. The Convent of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesus">Jesus</a>, designed by Master Diogo Boitaca, was built in the late 15th and early 16th centuries. John III granted the title of notable town in 1525. In 1580 the town supported Dom António, Prior of Crato, against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-ii-of-spain">Philip II of Spain</a> and was besieged by Spanish troops under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fernando-alvarez-de-toledo-3rd-duke-of-alba">Duke of Alba</a>; Philip later ordered the Fort of São Filipe, the work of Filippo Terzi. Salt made the 17th century the town's most prosperous age, and new walls were built after 1640. The earthquake of 1755 interrupted this prosperity.</p><p>The railway arrived in 1860, the year Pedro V made Setúbal a city, and sardine <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canning">canning</a>, oranges and muscatel wine made it known in the 19th century. It became the seat of a district in 1926 and of a diocese in 1975.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The municipality had 123,496 inhabitants at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> of 2021. Its main historical monument is the Monastery of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesus">Jesus</a>, a church of the 15th and 16th centuries that is one of the first buildings in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manueline-style">Manueline style</a>. The cathedral of Our Lady of Grace has a Mannerist façade, and the Fort of São Filipe overlooking the city is now a hotel. The port and its industries, among them paper, cement, fertilisers and shipbuilding, still tie the city to the sea.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Setúbal” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>At Tróia, near <b>Setúbal</b>, archaeologists have excavated one of the largest fish-salting complexes known in the Roman world, producing salted fish and sauces such as garum for export in locally made amphorae.</blockquote><a href="/empire/lusitania">Lusitania · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
